//! Sign-in, sign-out, password-reset and two-factor (TOTP) pages, generated
//! by Forge like Rails' authentication generator (ADR 0026, ADR 0040).
//! Change the pages and the wording freely; keep the security steps each
//! handler documents.
//!
//! | Method | Path | Page |
//! | --- | --- | --- |
//! | `GET` | `/session/new` | sign-in form: organization (tenant slug), email, password |
//! | `POST` | `/session` | sign in: `303` to the return path, `422` on any wrong field, `429` when throttled |
//! | `DELETE` | `/session` | sign out (the form sends `_method=delete`) |
//! | `GET` | `/passwords/new` | asks which account forgot its password |
//! | `POST` | `/passwords` | sends reset instructions; the same answer whether or not the account exists |
//! | `GET` | `/passwords/edit?token=` | the page a reset link opens |
//! | `PATCH` | `/passwords` | sets the new password and signs every session out |
//! | `GET` | `/session/mfa` | the second step of a sign-in: an authenticator or backup code |
//! | `POST` | `/session/mfa` | checks the code: `303` signed in, `422` wrong code, `429` throttled |
//! | `GET` | `/mfa` | two-factor settings of the signed-in account |
//! | `POST` | `/mfa` | turns it on after the password (step-up): shows the key to scan |
//! | `POST` | `/mfa/confirm` | confirms the key with the password and a code; shows the backup codes once |
//! | `POST` | `/mfa/rotation` | replaces the authenticator (password and current code) |
//! | `POST` | `/mfa/rotation/confirm` | confirms the new key (password, current code and a new code); shows new backup codes once |
//! | `POST` | `/mfa/backup_codes` | new backup codes (password and current code) |
//! | `DELETE` | `/mfa` | turns it off (password and current code) |
//!
//! How the flow stays safe:
//!
//! - Every form is CSRF-protected by the site's `CsrfProtection` layer, and
//!   each handler that changes credentials also refuses (`403`) a request
//!   the layer did not verify, so it fails closed without the layer.
//! - A wrong organization, email or password, a disabled account and an
//!   inactive membership all show one message (`422`); unknown logins still
//!   cost one Argon2id hash. Attempts are throttled per account and per
//!   network source (`429` with `Retry-After`); the source is
//!   `Request::client_ip`, the TCP peer unless it is a reverse proxy listed in
//!   `FORGE_HTTP_TRUSTED_PROXIES`.
//! - Signing in issues a new server-side session and a new CSRF token, both
//!   in fresh cookies, and revokes any session the browser already held, so
//!   an identifier or token planted before sign-in is useless after it.
//! - Return paths are validated as local paths when the page renders them,
//!   when the form posts them and before redirecting.
//! - Reset tokens are 256-bit, single-use, expire after 15 minutes, are bound
//!   to the account and the tenant they were requested through, are stored
//!   as SHA-256 digests and never logged. The reset pages are `no-store`
//!   with `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`. A reset revokes every session.
//! - Logins are canonical (`forge::auth::CanonicalLogin`: ASCII only,
//!   lowercased); anything else is an unknown account. Reset links go to
//!   the login as stored, never to the address typed into the form, and
//!   are issued and delivered by a bounded background queue while the
//!   page answers after a fixed minimum time, so timing does not reveal
//!   whether an account exists.
//! - Two-factor authentication (`forge::tenancy::MfaService`, ADR 0040): a
//!   right password for an account with a TOTP factor sets a five-minute
//!   challenge cookie (`__Host-forge_mfa`) instead of a session. The
//!   challenge is bound to the account's credential version, its factor and
//!   the browser's fresh CSRF token, counted per account and per source
//!   before any code is checked (`429` with `Retry-After`) and consumed with
//!   the code in one transaction: a code, or a backup code, works once.
//!   Without `FORGE_AUTH_MFA_KEY` such an account cannot sign in (an
//!   error page; the cause is logged).
//! - Turning two-factor on, and confirming its key, needs the password
//!   again; replacing the authenticator (and confirming the new key), new
//!   backup codes and turning it off need the password and a current code
//!   (step-up, throttled). The pending key is shown only after step-up. Turning it on, replacing it
//!   and turning it off sign the account out everywhere. Keys and backup
//!   codes are shown once, on `no-store` pages, and never logged.

